\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Simulation of UP-DOWN states, effect of serotonin and coupling strength. (a) Replication of UP and DOWN state dynamics. Without any serotonin stimulation, the activity in the brain regions UP and DOWN states. (b) Serotonin stimulation induces a DOWN state in several regions. Probability values between 0.5-1 seconds after the serotonin stimulation onset are included. (c) Direct comparison of the simulated and recorded DOWN state probability during serotonin stimulation. (d) In an unconnected network (G=0), the DRN projection density determines the strength of the effect of the modulation by serotonin. (e) Effect of coupling strength, demonstrated in example regions. The differently coloured graphs display different coupling strengths $G$ from $0-3$. (f) The serotonin effect was elevated in the visual cortex for increased coupling strength (p<0.05). (g) The difference between the $\Delta $ DOWN state probability for a coupling strength G=0 and G=3 is displayed for every region. The colour codes describe the average projection densities that each area receives from all other areas, indicating how strongly the areas are affected by the changes in coupling strength. }}{4}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:resultsummary1}{{3}{4}{Simulation of UP-DOWN states, effect of serotonin and coupling strength. (a) Replication of UP and DOWN state dynamics. Without any serotonin stimulation, the activity in the brain regions UP and DOWN states. (b) Serotonin stimulation induces a DOWN state in several regions. Probability values between 0.5-1 seconds after the serotonin stimulation onset are included. (c) Direct comparison of the simulated and recorded DOWN state probability during serotonin stimulation. (d) In an unconnected network (G=0), the DRN projection density determines the strength of the effect of the modulation by serotonin. (e) Effect of coupling strength, demonstrated in example regions. The differently coloured graphs display different coupling strengths $G$ from $0-3$. (f) The serotonin effect was elevated in the visual cortex for increased coupling strength (p<0.05). (g) The difference between the $\Delta $ DOWN state probability for a coupling strength G=0 and G=3 is displayed for every region. The colour codes describe the average projection densities that each area receives from all other areas, indicating how strongly the areas are affected by the changes in coupling strength}{figure.caption.5}{}}
